<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somali Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> Civil War is the armed conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> that grew out of resistance to the military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> in the 1980s and became general after his overthrow in 1991. Clan militias, foreign interventions, including the UN operations of 1992–1995 and Ethiopian forces, and later the Islamist group al-Shabaab have fought over a country that lacked an effective central government for much of the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somali-civil-war.jpg" alt="Somali Civil War" width="300" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Hawk_Down_Super64_over_Mogadishu_coast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of resistance to the military government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> during the 1980s. The defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> military by Cuban and Ethiopian forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war">Ogaden War</a> of 1977 to 1978 brought opposition within the armed forces to the surface, and more than a million refugees entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>. An attempted coup in April 1978, led mainly by officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majeerteen">Majeerteen</a> clan, was put down, and a former army commander, Abdullahi Yusuf, set up the Somali Salvation Democratic Front, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> created, trained and financed. The regime answered rebellion by rounding up hundreds of Majeerteen and by punitive measures against those it thought supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, especially in the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1988 to 1990 the Somali Armed Forces fought rebel groups including the Somali Salvation Democratic Front in the north-east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> in the north-west and the United Somali Congress in the south. By mid-1990 the United Somali Congress held most towns around Mogadishu, and it entered the city in December. By January 1991 it had defeated Barre's special forces, the Red Berets, and his government fell. The Somali National Movement controlled the north by February, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a> conference of April and May 1991 it declared an independent Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>.</p><p>In the south the factions of the congress commanders Mohamed Farah Aidid and Ali Mahdi Mohamed clashed over the capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> sent observers in July 1992 as UNOSOM I, followed by the UNITAF, a coalition led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, approved by Security Council Resolution 794 of 3 December 1992, and then UNOSOM II. After fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> insurgents and UNOSOM II troops in 1993, the United Nations withdrew in 1995. Aidid declared himself president on 15 June 1995 but received no recognition, and he died in August 1996 of wounds received in combat in Mogadishu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the withdrawal, local administrations and Islamic courts spread, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntland">Puntland</a> was established in 1998 beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-national-government">Transitional National Government</a> was set up in 2000 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-federal-government">Transitional Federal Government</a> in 2004. In 2006 Ethiopian troops invaded to depose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-courts-union">Islamic Courts Union</a> and install the transitional government, and a large insurgency followed in which al-Shabaab came to prominence.</p><p>Kenyan troops entered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in October 2011, and the Federal Government of Somalia was established in August 2012, the first permanent central government since the start of the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Somali Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
